            "text": "Every morning the city rehearses its small miracle: light slipping between buildings, buses sighing at curbs, and someone opening a bakery door before most people have remembered they are hungry.\n\nI like to imagine that each ordinary sound is part of an enormous, invisible orchestra. The traffic provides percussion; bicycle bells offer bright notes; footsteps keep time. Even the impatient horn has a place, though perhaps it is the orchestra\u2019s comic relief.\n\nSomewhere inside this daily composition, a child laughs at nothing, a dog pulls toward a fascinating leaf, and an old man pauses to watch clouds gather above the rooftops. These moments rarely announce themselves. They do not flash like advertisements or demand applause. They simply appear, then vanish, leaving behind a faint conviction that life is larger than our schedules.\n\nPerhaps attention is a quiet form of gratitude. To notice steam rising from coffee, rain darkening pavement, or sunlight resting briefly on a stranger\u2019s sleeve is to say: this exists, and I was here to see it. The world does not need our constant astonishment, but it may benefit from our occasional attention.\n\nWhen I forget this, I become efficient but strangely absent, moving through hours as if they were corridors built only for crossing. When I remember, even waiting in line can become a small window. Someone nearby is humming. The cashier is kind. Outside, a sparrow hops beneath a bench, fearless for reasons no human being could fully explain.\n\nFor a moment, nothing is missing. Absolutely."
